              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Evaluating confidence values**\n\nI'm noticing some odd confidence values from recommendations: B at .7714 and A at .6857. I wonder if these values arise from predicted probabilities based on all observed reports. It seems like B's high score might relate to its three highs observed, with a prior belief at 0.4. There's a possibility that these scores are linked to recommendations under survey conditions, suggesting we might want to explore probabilities rooted in Bayesian reasoning across peer forecasts.**Combining peer forecasts**\n\nI can treat peer forecasts as independent predictions and try to avoid double counting them since they come from different data sets. I think confidence can work as posterior probabilities, so I’ll combine their posterior odds with ours. When analyzing confidence levels, I need to remember that while they help, they may not always match calibrated likelihoods. Instead of averaging, I should consider multiplying the odds from independent observations, ensuring each peer report counts just once. Reconstructing all observations is important!**Counting observations accurately**\n\nI need to remember that each observation should only be counted once, rather than for each recommendation. This approach signals that I should combine survey observations effectively. Peer reports might be forecasts from different subsets, and confidence can help me understand their observed configurations better. I wonder if those peer reports were generated based on all observations. So, I should look for the relationship between a confidence score of .685715 and having three observations, especially if one is known.",
